1. PROJECT IDENTIFICATION
======================================================================

The Resilient Immutable Archive Pagination System (RIAPS) is an
architectural strategy and system design for constructing stable,
append-only archive pagination using immutable post-ID boundaries.

RIAPS is designed to address cascading archive invalidation associated
with conventional newest-first pagination systems.

In conventional pagination, the publication of a new item may shift
existing items between pagination pages, potentially changing a large
number of historical archive URLs even though only one new item was
created.

RIAPS instead constructs archive pages chronologically, appends qualifying
content to the currently active highest-numbered archive page, and seals
completed archive pages using persistent post-ID boundaries.

A central design principle of RIAPS is:

    Once an archive page is sealed, future publications or deletions
    should not cause unrelated content to cross that sealed page
    boundary.

The system is intended to enable, among other things:

    - stable historical archive pages;
    - localized cache invalidation;
    - reduced cascading pagination changes;
    - long-lived CDN and edge caching;
    - reduced SSR workload;
    - efficient indexed archive retrieval;
    - avoidance of large OFFSET-based pagination queries;
    - compact archive-boundary metadata;
    - scalable archival behavior for high-volume publishing systems.


